# Output and Change Discipline

These rules control how code changes should be delivered.

## Full File Delivery

When creating or changing a file, provide:

- The full file path.
- The full file contents.
- No omitted unchanged sections.
- No patch-only output.
- No diff-only output.

The user should be able to copy and paste the file directly.

## File Path Requirement

Always put the file path and filename before the file content.

Example:

`resources/views/blocks/example.blade.php`

Then provide the complete file.

## No Partial Snippets Unless Requested

Do not provide partial snippets unless the user explicitly asks for a snippet.

If the user asks to modify a file, return the complete modified file.

## Keep Changes Scoped

Make only the requested changes.

Do not perform unrelated cleanup.

Do not refactor unrelated code.

Do not rename files, classes, functions, CSS selectors, handles, variables, or fields unless the task requires it.

Do not reorganize markup unless the task requires it.

## Preserve Existing Work

If the user provides a file, treat that file as the source of truth.

Preserve existing:

- Structure
- Comments
- Naming
- Classes
- Hooks
- Filters
- Design tokens
- Conditionals
- Escaping
- Accessibility behavior

Only change what is needed for the requested task.

## Comments and Documentation

When outputting full files, keep comments useful.

Comment why major sections exist.

Do not over-comment obvious code.

Do not remove existing comments unless they are wrong or obsolete because of the requested change.

## Assumptions

If something is unknown, use a safe default and clearly mark the assumption.

Ask a question only when the missing information blocks the work.

When possible, continue with the safest minimal implementation.

## Final Response

After providing files, briefly summarize:

- What changed.
- Whether versioning was bumped.
- Whether changelog was updated.
- Any assumptions made.

Keep the summary short.